  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 PM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nA strengthening heat dome over the southern to southeast CONUS \nthe next few days will send hot and humid air into portions of \nLower Michigan. The unsettled \"ring of fire\" pattern returns at \nleast through Wednesday as a surface front wavers north then back \nsouth underneath upper-level shortwaves and jet streaks. Global \nensemble systems offer good confidence in heat and humidity \nbuilding through Wednesday, then fair confidence for some relief \nfrom the hotter temperatures for the latter part of the week. \nThough dependent on thunderstorms each day, high temperatures \naround 90 and a heat index climbing to 95-100 are possible in \nsouthwest and south-central Lower Michigan Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nThis evening, a midlevel vorticity center moving through southern\nWI and northern IL is touching off showers and thunderstorms. \nThis morning's models have not been enthusiastic about the \nthunderstorm potential in southwest Michigan this evening, given a\ncapped surface and poor midlevel lapse rates and limited CAPE \nenvironment that this disturbance will move into. But will \nmaintain showers and a slight chance of a thunderstorm for this \nevening mainly south of I-96.\n\nEarly Monday, the nose of a 850-700 mb jet will be pointed at \nwestern and central Lwr MI, where somewhat better but still modest\nCAPE around 500 J/kg will exist. This provides another chance of \nscattered thunderstorms, which will again be relatively high-based\nas most of the moisture will be above 800 mb and closer to 700 mb\nor 10,000 feet.\n\nConfidence in the timing of subsequent rounds of thunderstorms \nfrom Monday night to Wednesday is quite low, but models increase \nCAPE values, and adequate deep-layer shear will be in place much \nof the time. This will set up at least marginal threats for severe\nweather during this period.",
